WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.650
One day I was walking to school

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:03.830
when a man tried to force me into a car

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:05.580
where another man was waiting.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.960
I kicked and screamed.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.950
People on the streets rushed to help me.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.450
And they eventually let me go.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.250
My family and I left Iraq the next day.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.070
A local militia had threatened
the associated press

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:17.930
where my dad was a reporter.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.870
The militia was upset with
recent coverage of its activities

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.950
and ended up murdering his colleague

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.190
and lodging a bullet

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.280
in the head of his three-year-old son.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.730
After a shock of what
happened to me wore off,

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:33.030
my parents joked that
kidnappers changed their minds

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.760
after being exposed to what
I was like as a teenager.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.210
This is the kind of humor

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.250
that Iraqis are known for,

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:41.720
the darker, the better.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.440
And it's one reason that Ahmed Albasheer,

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.970
the country's most
influential media figure

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:49.830
has managed to capture
the hearts of Iraqis.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.680
Half of Iraq's total population

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.150
regularly tunes into his show.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.860
Albasheer was born and
raised in Ramadi, Iraq,

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.640
which experienced the
worst of the Iraq war.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.470
He lost several family members,

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.420
including his brother and father.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.780
In 2005, he was kidnapped

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.790
and tortured by a Shia militia.

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.450
16 years later,

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.220
Albasheer has built a
remarkably successful show

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.610
by criticizing the Iraqi
government and its corruption.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.320
I spoke to Albasheer at
the Oslo Freedom Forum,

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.290
a conference that gathers
media figures, activists,

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.240
and dissidents to talk
about government oppression

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.138
and human rights violations
all over the world.

00:01:27.138 --> 00:01:29.610
Albasheer and I talked
about the situation in Iraq

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.180
and the Iraqi elections.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.400
They say before Saddam,
we have one Saddam.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.030
Now we have a thousand Saddam.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.400
Every corner in Iraq,
there is a small dictator

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.660
and whoever says anything about him,

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.430
he would be killed publicly.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.310
I wanted to joke about
escaping my kidnappers,

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.150
but I thought it might be inappropriate

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:47.600
since he couldn't escape his.

00:01:50.071 --> 00:01:50.904
You think that was funny?

00:01:50.904 --> 00:01:51.737
I think that's funny.

00:01:51.737 --> 00:01:53.654
I think it's hilarious.

